        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>Quenching is the heat treatment process where steel is heated to a high temperature (above its critical point) and then rapidly cooled in water, oil, or air to increase its hardness and strength. This process locks the steel in a hardened state by preventing the formation of softer microstructures.</p>",
